The code uses the popular Credit Card Fraud Detection dataset, which contains transactions made by credit cards with features that are the result of a PCA transformation for privacy.
Supervised Learning (Random Forest Classifier)
I've implemented a Random Forest Classifier as the supervised learning technique. This is appropriate for fraud detection because:

It handles imbalanced data well
It's resistant to overfitting
It provides feature importance rankings

Unsupervised Learning (K-means Clustering)
For the unsupervised approach, I've used K-means clustering to identify natural groupings in the data without using the fraud labels. This helps detect anomalies and patterns that might not be captured by supervised methods.
Five Visualizations

Class Distribution - Bar chart showing the imbalance between fraudulent and legitimate transactions
Confusion Matrix - Heatmap displaying the model's prediction accuracy
Feature Importance - Bar chart of the top 10 most influential features for prediction
ROC Curve - Visualization of the classifier's performance across different thresholds
Cluster Visualization - Scatter plot showing how K-means clustering groups the transactions compared to actual fraud/non-fraud labels